hed Black's lead into the thirties. GPT-6.1 Sol averaged 4.8 points lost per move against the 20k's 3.4, and the bigger swings came late.", "moments": [{"turn": 9, "text": "The 20k's F6 lost 9.6 points and swung Black's lead from +6.2 to -3.4. It was a clear early gift to White, though the engine preferred C6."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The turning point. White led by 5.2 before this move. F7 lost 16.8 points and left Black ahead by 11.6, where the engine's choice was C6. White never led again."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "B2 lost 19.7 points, taking Black's lead from +12.6 to +32.2, while the engine's choice was D2. White's stones on the left side were already crowded against Black's A-file stones."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "The 20k answered with A2, which lost 21.4 points and cut the lead from +32.2 to +10.8. It looked like a lifeline for White, but G8 was the engine's choice."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "B1 was GPT-6.1 Sol's worst move, losing 23.9 points and sending Black's lead from +10.8 to +34.8. D2 was the engine's choice. The game was effectively decided here, and the final B+34 is close to that +34.8."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every script either listed tools, read notes, played a move the player had already chosen via mcp__go__play, or ran inspectLiberties from current-position-liberties.js, which only parses the current board text and lists chains and liberties. The remaining commands built an SGF from the game history and wrote the game notes, and none of them generated, scored or ranked candidate moves or looked ahead.", "stop": false}}